{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1663 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "a49d4e21c1d1879bc7f8f64618e3b15d583412865e4d2ff1abd1635ad3e70673" :edb/db-before-t 1662 :edb/db-after-t 1663 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534975 50 #inst "2026-10-08T12:49:39.383Z" 13194139534975 true] [17592186197940 1100 "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v))$. The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y)\\to(u(x,y), v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ in terms of $u$ and $v$." 13194139534975 false] [17592186197940 1100 "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y)\\to(u(x,y), v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/2614cbd879ea1014caf69d1239b69e81ebefcc76b8946962092cb83cc96dc987.png)\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" 13194139534975 true] [17592186197940 1145 "Given a transformation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}$. So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)}$, the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}$.\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n$\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1}$.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nu(x,y) = e^{x+y}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nv(x,y) = e^{x-y}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} &= e^{x + y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y}|= e^{x + y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x}|= e^{x - y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}|=-e^{x - y}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(u, v))/(∂(x, y)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}e^{x + y} & e^{x + y} \\\\ e^{x - y} & -e^{x - y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= e^{x + y} \\cdot (-e^{x - y}) - e^{x + y} \\cdot e^{x - y} \\\\\n&=-e^{x + y} \\cdot e^{x - y} - e^{x + y} \\cdot e^{x - y} \\\\\n&=-2 \\cdot e^{x + y} \\cdot e^{x - y} \\\\\n&=-2uv\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= ((∂(u, v))/(∂(x, y)))^{-1} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2uv}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534975 false] [17592186197940 1145 "Given a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/149438cd70059230993ca93a2122158f71edb665b3948cbd4ff3a9c038d7e48f.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y) = e^{x+y}$ and $v(x,y) = e^{x-y}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & ={e}^{x-y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y} & {e}^{x+y} \\\\ {e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x+y}\\cdot (-{e}^{x-y})-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y}-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2uv}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534975 true] [17592186197970 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/6e97c59a7c736ec70142bc402566385fc2cd9c6aa78e304d70cefab1027078c1.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=0$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=-3.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem?\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=-3$\n\n- $f^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=0$\n\n- The derivative of $f^{-1}(y)$ at $y=-1.1$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)}$" 13194139534975 true] [17592186197970 1100 "The function $f(x)$, whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=0$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=-3$. Which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4149/Source/Images/af9f81048aa4f33fd0c1b0f79d35ca30.png)\n\n1. $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=-3$\n2. $f^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=0$\n3. The derivative of $f^{-1}(y)$ at $y=-1.1$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)}$" 13194139534975 false] [17592186197970 1145 "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f: U \\to \\Bbb R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ $f(a) = b$ and $f'(a)\\neq 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *$f^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of $f^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(b) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(a)} =\\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $f$ is not continuous at $x = -3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement II is also false. Notice that $y = 0 = f(0).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x = 0$ since $f'(0) = 0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $y = -1.1 = f(2),$ and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x = 2.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=2,$ for example, $(0,4),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x = 2,$ i.e., $f'(2) \\neq 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, the derivative of $f^{-1}(y)$ at $y = -1.1$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534975 true] [17592186197970 1145 "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n> *If $f: U \\to \\Bbb R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R$, $f(a) = b$ and $f'(a)\\neq 0$ for $a\\in U$, then*\n> - *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a$,*\n> - *$f^{-1}$, the inverse of $f$, is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b$, and*\n> - *the derivative of $f^{-1}$ is given by*\n> $\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(b) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(a)} =\\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(b))}\\,$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $f$ is not continuous at $x = -3$. So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n- Statement II is also false. Notice that $y = 0 = f(0)$. However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x = 0$ since $f'(0) = 0$. So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $y = -1.1 = f(2)$, and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x = 2$. Indeed,$f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=2$, for example, $(0,4)$, andthe derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x = 2$, i.e., $f'(2) \\neq 0$.Therefore, by the inverse function theorem, the derivative of $f^{-1}(y)$ at $y = -1.1$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534975 false] [17592186198161 1100 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, where $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534975 false] [17592186198161 1100 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf T$ is defined as \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/4a20bc6cf78708f70fb1a696b9dc5971feef974df6429fa4169f82881c6d22e0.png)" 13194139534975 true] [17592186198161 1145 "Given a transformation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $\\mathbf T$, we need to solve\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = 0\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nx(u,v) = u^2-v^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny(u,v) = u+v\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} &= 2u & \\frac{∂x}{∂v}|=-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u}|= 1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}|= 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}2u & -2v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2(u + v)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} =0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= 0 \\\\\n2(u + v) &= 0 \\\\\nu &= -v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $T$ consist of those points that lie on the line $u=-v$ in the $uv$-plane." 13194139534975 false] [17592186198161 1145 "Given a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/62dec67282c5bc2707377cb976412a48ca3c41ffeed6aa74ef0d5b1ef8d6e380.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $\\mathbf T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}  = 0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = u^2-v^2$ and $y(u,v) = u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -2v \\\\ \\,\\,1\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(u+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} =0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2(u+v) & =0 \\\\ u & =-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $T$ consist of those points that lie on the line $u=-v$  in the $uv$-plane." 13194139534975 true] [17592186198287 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324040 13194139534975 true] [17592186198287 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324047 13194139534975 true] [17592186198287 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324054 13194139534975 true] [17592186198287 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324116 13194139534975 true] [17592186198297 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324061 13194139534975 true] [17592186198297 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324068 13194139534975 true] [17592186198297 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324123 13194139534975 true] [17592186198297 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324130 13194139534975 true] [17592186198327 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324075 13194139534975 true] [17592186198327 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324082 13194139534975 true] [17592186198327 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324089 13194139534975 true] [17592186198327 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324096 13194139534975 true] [17592186198327 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324103 13194139534975 true] [17592186198928 1017 #edb/ref 17592186197803 13194139534975 false] [17592186198928 1017 #edb/ref 17592186324110 13194139534975 true] [17592186198928 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534975 true] [17592186198928 1100 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T$, where $T$ is defined as\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}uv \\\\ u + v\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139534975 false] [17592186198928 1100 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}uv \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534975 true] [17592186198928 1145 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=uv$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v\\cdot 1-u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ v-u & =0 \\\\ v & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=u$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane." 13194139534975 true] [17592186198929 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534975 true] [17592186198929 1100 "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$. The inverse transformation $T^{-1}(x, y) → (u(x, y), v(x, y))$ is given by\n\n$T^{-1}: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2xy \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v$." 13194139534975 false] [17592186198929 1100 "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2xy \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" 13194139534975 true] [17592186198929 1145 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2xy$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =2x \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2y\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-2x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4v}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324040 1017 #edb/ref 17592186324041 13194139534975 true] [17592186324040 1089 "q-160460" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324040 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534975 true] [17592186324040 1100 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324040 1113 #uuid "b086e929-2933-5923-87f3-a40760986e35" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324040 1145 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -2v \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-(-2v)\\cdot v \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2({u}^{2}+{v}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=0$ defines the point $(0,0)$ in the $uv$-plane." 13194139534975 true] [17592186324041 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324042 13194139534975 true] [17592186324041 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324043 13194139534975