7592186324054 1145 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=u{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}+2v.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2uv \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & 2uv \\\\ 2u & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={v}^{2}\\cdot 2-2uv\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{v}^{2}-4{u}^{2}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{v}^{2}-4{u}^{2}v & =0 \\\\ v(2v-4{u}^{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v(2v-4{u}^{2})=0$ defines the line $v=0,$ i.e., the $u$-axis, and the parabola $v=2{u}^{2}$ in the $uv$-plane." 13194139534975 true] [17592186324055 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324056 13194139534975 true] [17592186324055 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324057 13194139534975 true] [17592186324055 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324058 13194139534975 true] [17592186324055 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324059 13194139534975 true] [17592186324055 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324060 13194139534975 true] [17592186324055 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534975 true] [17592186324055 1041 "selection" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324055 1104 #edb/ref 17592186324056 13194139534975 true] [17592186324055 1110 #uuid "7b94ee39-c4fb-5827-a1f0-aa4a945374d9" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324056 1034 "The critical points lie on the line $v=0$ and on the parabola $v=2{u}^{2}.$" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324056 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534975 true] [17592186324056 1111 #uuid "194e4843-dc33-5afd-bdc7-3ac1c1ef2ea1" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324057 1034 "The critical points lie on the lines $u=-2v$ and $u=v.$" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324057 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534975 true] [17592186324057 1111 #uuid "b2687845-7270-5e6b-bf80-18af3c3faf92" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324058 1034 "The critical points lie on the line $v=0$ and on the hyperbola ${v}^{2}-{u}^{2}.$" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324058 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534975 true] [17592186324058 1111 #uuid "0c35f47a-8ebc-57fa-9c24-5e64620a4a6d" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324059 1034 "The critical points lie on the parabola $u={v}^{2}.$" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324059 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534975 true] [17592186324059 1111 #uuid "46631a05-f41c-598d-8043-806b1f53599c" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324060 1034 "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324060 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534975 true] [17592186324060 1111 #uuid "07e59a61-c9e7-5b88-94a0-151cf98656dc" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324061 1017 #edb/ref 17592186324062 13194139534975 true] [17592186324061 1089 "q-160563" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324061 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534975 true] [17592186324061 1100 "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-6y \\\\ x+3y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324061 1113 #uuid "aec613c2-8b31-57c1-8b1d-7f35687a5b36" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324061 1145 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2x+-y$ and $v(x,y)=x+3y.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-6 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-(-6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324062 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324063 13194139534975 true] [17592186324062 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324064 13194139534975 true] [17592186324062 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324065 13194139534975 true] [17592186324062 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324066 13194139534975 true] [17592186324062 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324067 13194139534975 true] [17592186324062 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534975 true] [17592186324062 1041 "selection" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324062 1104 #edb/ref 17592186324067 13194139534975 true] [17592186324062 1110 #uuid "cf47f4f6-db5d-5d60-94e6-b528e52c0e24" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324063 1034 "12" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324063 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534975 true] [17592186324063 1111 #uuid "a801c113-56c8-5b89-82db-87c58217ea10" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324064 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324064 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534975 true] [17592186324064 1111 #uuid "676eba50-edb9-56af-bdad-5b0d436ae887" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324065 1034 "2" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324065 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534975 true] [17592186324065 1111 #uuid "c94f8e40-fc3c-598a-b89d-862c2821fdce" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324066 1034 "6" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324066 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534975 true] [17592186324066 1111 #uuid "489d6705-792c-5948-bc51-d0f64ec6adf7" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324067 1034 "\\frac{1}{12}" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324067 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534975 true] [17592186324067 1111 #uuid "1466b790-196d-5e83-b022-527990c5f9ba" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324068 1017 #edb/ref 17592186324069 13194139534975 true] [17592186324068 1089 "q-160581" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324068 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534975 true] [17592186324068 1100 "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-x+7y \\\\ x+3y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324068 1113 #uuid "9e6a6139-c56b-5ec6-8b4e-5f56e91ad2e0" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324068 1145 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=-x+7y$ and $v(x,y)=3x-y.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =7 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 3-7\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324069 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324070 13194139534975 true] [17592186324069 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324071 13194139534975 true] [17592186324069 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324072 13194139534975 true] [17592186324069 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324073 13194139534975 true] [17592186324069 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324074 13194139534975 true] [17592186324069 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534975 true] [17592186324069 1041 "selection" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324069 1104 #edb/ref 17592186324070 13194139534975 true] [17592186324069 1110 #uuid "2530e453-37b5-5e8b-a1d1-14494d277462" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324070 1034 "-\\frac{1}{10}" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324070 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534975 true] [17592186324070 1111 #uuid "84194ed3-152e-51ef-9fed-4d26e5b97dba" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324071 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324071 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534975 true] [17592186324071 1111 #uuid "d85a72eb-8030-543e-884a-73d01b31b02f" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324072 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324072 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534975 true] [17592186324072 1111 #uuid "ee2f2ebf-ed3f-5d89-be74-95d5dbc33054" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324073 1034 "4" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324073 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534975 true] [17592186324073 1111 #uuid "96bc3664-62f5-58c7-9359-a5e0bddaf40b" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324074 1034 "-\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324074 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534975 true] [17592186324074 1111 #uuid "9c59a974-ba57-5f3c-af4c-5070b0609f93" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324075 1017 #edb/ref 17592186324076 13194139534975 true] [17592186324075 1089 "q-161845" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324075 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534975 true] [17592186324075 1100 "Let $f:U\\to R$ be continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R$ such that $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- The function $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n- The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324075 1113 #uuid "1936251c-b682-5e21-bc78-b5e80d9892c5" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324075 1145 "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the theorem, the function $f$ must be invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Indeed, the inverse ${f}^{-1}$ must be continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\n- Statement III is also true. According to the theorem, we must have ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324076 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324077 13194139534975 true] [17592186324076 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324078 13194139534975 true] [17592186324076 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324079 13194139534975 true] [17592186324076 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324080 13194139534975 true] [17592186324076 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324081 13194139534975 true] [17592186324076 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534975 true] [17592186324076 1041 "selection" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324076 1104 #edb/ref 17592186324077 13194139534975 true] [17592186324076 1110 #uuid "d97d62fd-b53d-5eea-a97f-b50e17a3172a" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324077 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324077 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534975 true] [17592186324077 1111 #uuid "9ae1a655-99be-5655-a3be-505d223f0a32" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324078 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324078 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534975 true] [17592186324078 1111 #uuid "042f64df-fc71-5aeb-870e-58020f56f1fa" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324079 1034 "I only" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324079 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534975 true] [17592186324079 1111 #uuid "6ffd7393-b068-5965-ab30-48cd53a98bac" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324080 1034 "III only" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324080 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534975 true] [17592186324080 1111 #uuid "fd173bfa-2606-5ec8-a9a8-f67b2d42062f" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324081 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324081 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534975 true] [17592186324081 1111 #uuid "6223297b-3f1f-5765-a0be-fad3e5b45c5e" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324082 1017 #edb/ref 17592186324083 13194139534975 true] [17592186324082 1089 "q-161857" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324082 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534975 true] [17592186324082 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/0a79260df29825684d6bdf6197df8581adf5a5a2d1d89938c299f18b3f33bf7c.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=2$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=3.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-2$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=2.91$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)}$" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324082 1113 #uuid "0bb25d39-9aff-5295-b9a5-be77270cb4ba" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324082 1145 "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=1.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=1,$ for example, $(0,2),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=1,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(1)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, $f$ is invertible in *some* neighborhood of $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $y=-2=f(2).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=2$ since ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $y=2.91=f(3),$ but $f$ is not continuous at $x=3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324083 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324084 13194139534975 true] [17592186324083 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324085 13194139534975 true] [17592186324083 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324086 13194139534975 true] [17592186324083 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324087 13194139534975 true] [17592186324083 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324088 13194139534975 true] [17592186324083 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534975 true] [17592186324083 1041 "selection" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324083 1104 #edb/ref 17592186324085 13194139534975 true] [17592186324083 1110 #uuid "655e28ae-f320-5522-a2dd-7a0b4bf9b7b0" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324084 1034 "None of the statements are true" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324084 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534975 true] [17592186324084 1111 #uuid "94c04578-6691-5740-b87f-bfc4e34d68b5" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324085 1034 "I only" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324085 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534975 true] [17592186324085 1111 #uuid "8eece770-d44b-5537-bfbf-470cb187e0e2" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324086 1034 "III only" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324086 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534975 true] [17592186324086 1111 #uuid "82038214-af23-5d92-b605-c479872b7f67" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324087 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534975 true] [17592186324087 1103 #edb/ref 175921860